VT_TALK("sql/sql-keys", {
 "why": "Picture one giant table with every order, and the customer details typed again in each row. Asha orders three times, so her email appears three times. If she changes it, someone must fix all three rows, and if one is missed, the database disagrees with itself. A new customer with no orders has nowhere to live, and deleting someone's only order deletes the customer too. The cure is to give each kind of thing its own table, customers, products and orders, and connect them with keys, so each fact lives in exactly one place. In the animation, an order stores only a customer number, and following it leads to one customer. Think of a school register and a fee book that share roll numbers.",
 "keys": "A primary key is the column, or group of columns, that picks out exactly one row. It must be unique and must never be NULL, and a table has only one. Any column that could do the job is a candidate key. A natural key comes from the real world, like an ISBN. A surrogate key is a plain number the database makes up, like id one, two, three, and most designers prefer it because real world values change. A composite key uses two columns together, like order id and product id in the order items table. In SQLite, a column declared exactly INTEGER PRIMARY KEY becomes the hidden row number. Think of a roll number that stays the same even if a student changes their name.",
 "fk": "A foreign key is a promise that a value in one table matches a real row in another. Every order's customer id must be the id of an existing customer. The database checks this promise on every change. Try the first block, inserting an order for customer ninety nine fails, because that customer does not exist. Try the second, deleting Asha fails, because her orders still point at her. You choose what happens on delete: refuse, cascade the delete to the children, or set their link to NULL. One SQLite detail, foreign keys are off by default, and every connection must switch them on with a pragma, which these pages already do. Think of a library that will not lend a book to a card number nobody owns.",
 "rel": "Tables relate in three shapes. One to many is the most common, one customer has many orders, and the foreign key goes on the many side. Many to many, like orders and products, needs a junction table in the middle that holds pairs, here order items with an order id and a product id. One to one, like a person and a passport, uses a foreign key that is also unique. Designers draw all this as an entity relationship diagram, an idea Peter Chen introduced in 1976. In crow's foot notation, a three pronged foot means many and a bar means one. Think of a school where one class has many students, and students and clubs meet through a club membership list.",
 "levels": "Up the ladder. For a child, tables are boxes of one kind of thing, and keys are name tags and arrows. At college level, referential integrity means every foreign key points at a real parent or is NULL, and actions like cascade decide what happens to children. At masters level, a foreign key can be deferred, so it is checked only at commit, and you should index the child column so deleting a parent stays fast. At research level, huge databases spread over many servers sometimes skip foreign key checks across machines, because asking another server is slow. Think of a postal address, it only works if the house really exists at the end of it."
});
